r its handling of the economy and a  raging pandemic  others fear it's an unconstitutional  power grab  that could lead to a dictatorship a move  that would see the end to the jasmine  revolution  that paved the way to a decade of  democracy  laura burden manly al jazeera let's  cross over to muhammad vallar  correspondent who's covered  tunisia extensively for al jazeera  muhammad it seems that the  turnaround you might say by uh hanada  has is  been quite swift and quite surprising  yeah that's right so i think another has  realized that  the the conditions on the ground are not  in their favor or in the favor of their  call  for more demonstrations they have seen  those decrees  taken by the president yesterday and  they have seen the army  uh from you know ranging its stance  range  ranging from neutral to to pro president  the army ready to protect the um the  decisions and make  make sure they are implemented the army  has been guarding the  uh the government buildings and  preventing chaos from happening or  preventing confrontations  so those decisions were a strong message  from the president yesterday to the  people of tunisia and another party has  received us that message nada has a  history of uh  moderation as well in their in their  stances whenever there is a huge crisis  in tunisia  and probably that's one of the reasons  why tunisia has been able to avoid  um the fate of uh of egypt and other  kind of  other arab spring countries in 2013 2014  another gave major concessions  in order to make sure that the national  dialogue could reach a  national consensus or a reconciliation  they also in 2016 another decided to  to um to abandon its policy of  islamic proselytizing as a part of its  political ideology  and it reduced itself into a political  party so they have a history  of this kind of of moves to appease  whenever there is a an escalation and  now they have  done it again and but they are very  resilient apparently and they want to  continue t